// ============================================================================
// CALL-EDGE-GEN · CLIENT CALL EDGES  (provenance-split resolved edges)
//
// Mirrors java/engine/call-edge-generation/calls.dl: consume the disambiguated callee
// set and split it by whether the callee is CLIENT or LIBRARY code, so a row is
// self-describing.
//
// WHAT IS NOT PORTED, and why. Java's lib-boundary gate (lib_shadowed_for /
// lib_target_kept) exists because method_name_in_type hands callee-resolution EVERY
// declaration of a name reachable from the receiver's type, and Java binds exactly the
// NEAREST one. Python's equivalent narrowing happens EARLIER and differently: the MRO's
// prec-minimality (resolution/attribute-lookup.dl) already selects the winner, and it
// does so on an ORDER rather than on a subtype test — which is the whole reason C3 is
// needed. So there is nothing left to suppress at boundary-emission time, and a copy of
// Java's gate here would be dead code pretending to be a safeguard.
// ============================================================================

// ── A .pyi DECLARATION IS NOT AN EDGE TARGET (issue #223) ────────────────────
// A stub is not importable — CPython's SOURCE_SUFFIXES is ['.py'] — so no function
// object exists at a name it declares and no execution can ever reach one. An edge to a
// stub is therefore outside every sound envelope: not wide, WRONG. Measured before this
// gate: `from accel import fast; fast(1)` with only accel.pyi present came out
// `known_edge -> accel.fast`, a committed single target in a file that raises
// ModuleNotFoundError on import. Where a real module sits beside the stub, the stub
// survived as a redundant second candidate and the site stopped being exact.
//
// THE GATE IS ON THE EDGE, NOT ON CANDIDATE ADMISSION, and that difference is the whole
// design of this change. A .pyi is the best source of ANNOTATIONS in the tree — it is
// what a stub is for — and the engine already uses them: `fast(1).bit_length()` resolves
// to builtin:int.bit_length off the stub's `-> int`, verified before and after. Dropping
// the stub at candidate admission would have taken that with it, because
// call_returns_type reads expr_call_candidate. So the method stays a candidate and a
// type source, and only stops being something a call EDGE can commit to.
method_declared_in_stub(p, m) :- method_module(p, mod, m), module_is_stub(p, mod).

// ── client_calls_client(CallerProv, SiteHash, CalleeProv, CalleeMethodHash) ──
client_calls_client("client", site, "client", callee) :-
    expr_resolves_to_method(site, callee),
    method_owner("client", _, callee),
    !method_declared_in_stub("client", callee).
// Module-level and nested functions have no owning class, so method_owner does not
// cover them — and they are 16% of functions plus every closure. Java has no analogue
// of this clause because Java has no code outside a class.
client_calls_client("client", site, "client", callee) :-
    expr_resolves_to_method(site, callee),
    method_module_level("client", _, callee),
    !method_declared_in_stub("client", callee).

// ── AND THE SITE IS NAMED, NOT SHRUGGED AT ──────────────────────────────────
// Suppressing the target alone would leave the site ambiguous_unknown, which says "the
// analysis has a blind spot here". It does not: the callee is known exactly, and the
// reason it cannot be followed is that its implementation is not Python. That is the
// same argument resolution/builtin-types.dl makes for list.append, and it gets the same
// treatment — a NAMED boundary. `external:` because no entity for the implementation
// exists anywhere in the fact base, which is exactly what the label means here.
// ONLY WHERE THE STUB IS ALL THAT IS LEFT. A package that ships impl.py and impl.pyi
// side by side resolves to the real function, and the call does NOT leave the Python
// surface — labelling it external there would be a second, contradictory answer on an
// exact site. Measured on that fixture: with this gate the co-located site is a single
// known_edge to impl.compute, which is now EXACT rather than the two-member
// multi_inferred it was while the stub counted as a target.
call_hits_external(site, cat("external:", q)) :-
    expr_resolves_to_method(site, callee),
    method_declared_in_stub("client", callee),
    method_decl("client", _, _, q, _, callee),
    !site_client_target(site, _).

// ── client_calls_lib(CallerProv, SiteHash, CalleeProv, LibMethodHash) ────────
// Reached by NAME, never by hash (resolution/lib-linking.dl). Not expanded: the chain
// ENDS at the library in a client-only build.
client_calls_lib("client", site, "lib", callee) :- call_hits_lib_method(site, callee).
// AND BY HASH, once the library's BODIES are staged (#375). The clause above is the
// NAME route: an import is linked to a library declaration and the site commits to it.
// It cannot reach a callable the library never exports under a name the client wrote —
// `plain.<locals>.wrapper`, the closure a library decorator returns, is reached only by
// resolving the decorated name through the library's own return flow, and that answer
// arrives as a METHOD HASH in expr_resolves_to_method like any other.
//
// Without this the whole chain resolved and then had nowhere to go: decorated_name_target
// named the library closure correctly, expr_resolves_to_method carried it to the edge
// layer, and client_calls_client dropped it because method_owner("client", ...) does not
// hold for a library method. The site came out ambiguous_unknown with the answer already
// computed one relation upstream.
client_calls_lib("client", site, "lib", callee) :-
    expr_resolves_to_method(site, callee), method_owner("lib", _, callee).
client_calls_lib("client", site, "lib", callee) :-
    expr_resolves_to_method(site, callee), method_module_level("lib", _, callee).

// ── client_calls_builtin(CallerProv, SiteHash, TargetLabel) ──────────────────
// A CPython C-level callable: `super()`, `object.__init__` for a class with no
// constructor anywhere in its MRO. The callee is known exactly and has no Python source
// in ANY IR, so the target is a LABEL, not a hash — deliberately not hash-shaped, so it
// can never be mistaken for a py_method FK.
client_calls_builtin("client", site, label) :- call_hits_builtin(site, label).

// ── client_calls_external(SiteKey, TargetLabel) ──────────────────────────────
// A callee named by an import path that resolved neither in-repo nor against the staged
// library — a C extension, or a dependency this build did not load
// (resolution/lib-linking.dl). Labelled `external:<written path>`, which is a NAME and
// not a hash, because no entity for it exists anywhere in the fact base.
client_calls_external(site, label) :- call_hits_external(site, label).
// A library was staged and consulted but produced no member: still a NAMED boundary,
// never a silent drop (issue #72).
client_calls_external(site, label) :- call_hits_lib_unresolved(site, label).
